#idx("Training course, phase 6")Phase 6 of the training course teaches you to work with the #term("AWACS") (@awacs-requests) and the #term("tanker") (@tanker). The steps of each #term("lesson") are in its briefing and on the #term("Notebook")'s Lesson tab; the refuelling procedure is in each jet's manual: the #other("f16i", "air-refuelling"), the #other("f16d", "air-refuelling") and the #other("f35i", "air-refuelling").

#grid-table(
  (auto, 1fr, 1fr),
  ([Lesson], [What it teaches], [How it is graded]),
  [6.1 Check-in and the picture], [The check-in, the picture and the bogey dope.], [The check-in is your first request to the AWACS.],
  [6.2 Vectors], [Flying the AWACS's vectors to a contact, the hand-off (Judy), the vector home.], [The check-in; your heading 30 to 90 seconds after each vector, within 5°, 10° or 20° of it on average.],
  [6.3 Air refuelling], [The vector to the tanker, the join-up, contact, fuel to full, the disconnect.], [The time from 1 nautical mile behind the tanker to contact (2, 4 or 6 minutes); the unplanned disconnects (none, one or two); the fuel taken of the room in your tanks (all, 90 % or 75 %).],
  [6.4 At night], [Air refuelling by night, on the tanker's lights.], [As 6.3.],
  [6.5 Check ride 6], [Everything of phases 1 to 6 but the formation phase.], [Every item of those phases' lessons.],
)

The three limits of each standard are those of Above average, Average and Below average; past the last the item is Unsatisfactory. Check ride 6 is flown by day, without the instructor's calls in flight; the notebook stays with you, and one emergency already taught may come at a random time, unannounced.
